<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Regions</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xiyu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Regions was the Chinese name, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, for the lands west of the Yumen Pass, chiefly the oasis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> such as Kucha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Karasahr</a>, Khotan and Shule. Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> contested them with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> from the 2nd century BCE and set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of the Western Regions in 60 BCE; the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-chao">Ban Chao</a> reconquered them between the 70s and 90s CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-regions.jpg" alt="Western Regions" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: トムル · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Regions_1st_century_BC(en).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To control trade along the Silk Road the Uyghurs allied with the Sogdian merchants who controlled oases of Central Asia; the Sogdians enabled them to trade in the <b>Western Regions</b> and exchange Chinese silk for other goods, and their trading communities in China gained Uyghur protection in return.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…search for them also opened China to the West: Zhang Qian's mission, though it failed to secure an alliance, gave the Han court its first detailed knowledge of Fergana, Sogdiana, Bactria, Parthia and India, and encouraged Emperor Wu of Han to push Chinese power into the <b>Western Regions</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Han dynasty, campaigning from 108 BCE, set up the Protectorate of the <b>Western Regions</b> in 60 BCE, using plots against local rulers, attacks such as that on Kucha in 65 BCE, and the massacre of the whole population of Luntai when it resisted.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>It enters Chinese records in the 2nd century BCE as one of the city-states of the <b>Western Regions</b>, alternating between Xiongnu and Han control.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-karasahr">Kingdom of Karasahr</a></li><li><blockquote>Kucha, Qiuci in Chinese, was an Indo-European-speaking oasis state between the Tianshan Mountains and the Taklamakan desert, recorded from the 2nd century BCE among the kingdoms of the <b>Western Regions</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kucha">Kingdom of Kucha</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 70s CE the Han general Ban Chao used it as his base for reconquering the <b>Western Regions</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shule">Kingdom of Shule</a></li></ul></section>
